/** @page environment_variables Environment Variables

    This section will outline the environment variables that are used by %Qore.

    <b>%Qore Environment Variables</b>
    |!Environment Variable|!Description
    |\c QORE_AUTO_MODULE_DIR|This environment variable should contain a colon-separated list of directories which will be searched for %Qore modules when %Qore starts. If any modules are found in any of these directories, they are loaded automatically before any parsing starts.
    |\c QORE_MODULE_DIR|This environment variable should contain a colon-separated list of directories which will be searched when modules are loaded with the @ref requires "%requires" parse directive
    |\c QORE_MODULE_DIR_ONLY|If set, automatic module-path initialization uses only directories in \c QORE_MODULE_DIR and does not append the compiled-in default module directories. Applications that configure the module path explicitly through @ref Qore::ModuleManager before initialization retain their explicitly configured path. Per-Program paths from @ref prepend-module-path "%prepend-module-path" and @ref append-module-path "%append-module-path" remain active. This is useful for hermetic build and test environments.
    |\c QORE_VERIFY_MODULE_SIGNATURES|If set (to any value other than \c 0), the module loader verifies that a binary module file is intact before mapping it, and reports a \c LOAD-MODULE-ERROR naming the file if it is not. Off by default because the check hashes the whole file (measured at roughly 5 ms/MB on macOS), which a process loading many modules would pay on every start. It is worth turning on when a module load fails inexplicably: on macOS/Apple Silicon the kernel terminates a process that maps a page whose code signature no longer validates, so a \c .qmod damaged on disk kills the loading process inside \c dlopen() with no diagnostic at all, and this check turns that into an ordinary error. Currently implemented on macOS only; a no-op elsewhere, where a damaged module makes \c dlopen() fail normally.
    |\c QORE_INCLUDE_DIR|This variable should be a colon-separated list of directories where the %Qore binary should look for include files requested with the @ref include "%include" parse directive (deprecated for user modules in this repository)
    |\c QORE_CHARSET|If this variable is set, then the default character encoding name for the process will be the value of this variable. This variable takes precedence over the \c LANG variable, but can be overridden by the command line using option \c --charset (see @ref character_encoding for more information on this option)
    |\c LANG|If this variable is set and includes a character encoding specification, then, if the \c QORE_CHARSET variable is not set (and no character encoding was specified on the command line), this character encoding will be the default for the process.
    |\c TZ|On UNIX systems, this environment variables points to the zoneinfo file that contains the definition of the current time zone; for more information, see @ref unix_time_zones
    |\c QORE_DIAG_FORMAT|If set to \c json, %Qore parses the program and emits its parse diagnostics as a JSON array on \c stdout instead of executing it, exactly as if @ref diag_json_format "--diag-format=json" had been given on the command line. Intended for editors, build tools, and AI coding assistants that drive the \c qore binary but cannot control its command line.
    |\c QORE_CODE_FRAMES|If set (to any value), %Qore parses the program and renders its parse diagnostics as @ref diag_code_frames "code frames" on \c stderr instead of executing it, exactly as if \c --code-frames had been given on the command line.
    |\c QORE_JIT_OPT_LEVEL|Sets the process-wide LLVM optimization level used for @ref exec_mode_jit "JIT" native code generation: \c 0 (no optimization) to \c 3 (most aggressive); the default is \c 3, matching the default of the \c qcc AOT compiler. An invalid or empty value is ignored and the default is used. An individual %Program can override this with @ref Qore::Program::setJitOptimizationLevel() "Program::setJitOptimizationLevel()"; see @ref jit_optimization_level
    |\c QCC_JOBS|Read by the \c qcc AOT compiler: number of parallel backend-codegen threads for @ref qcc_parallel_codegen "split-after-opt" compilation (default: CPU count, capped at 32; \c 1 = single-object). Throttled by the GNU make jobserver when present. The \c qcc \c --jobs=N option sets this and takes precedence.
    |\c QCC_SPLIT_THRESHOLD|Read by the \c qcc AOT compiler: minimum optimized-IR instruction count for a module to be split for @ref qcc_parallel_codegen "parallel codegen" (default: \c 50000). Smaller modules use the single-object path.
*/
